> > I do not think that this exact bundling schema would be impacted negatively (as this PR would also get around the dual package hazard), but I imagine there might be more reasons to have something "not for node" in module.

"not for node" in module would be smth like this 😉 :
```ts
{
  "exports": {
    "module": {
      "node": "./dist/for-node.js",
      "default": "./dist/not-for-node.js"
    }
  }
}
```

I did some testing with the dual modules from the top 5000 high-impact npm packages using data from https://github.com/wooorm/npm-esm-vs-cjs/ and I think at least for a semver-minor feature, we do need to use a different name than "module", because I found several packages that point the `module` conditions to files that expected the module resolution to work in CJS style (and supported by the bundlers they use), even though the Node.js ESM loader intentionally chose to differ from CJS and does not support these when resolving imports in ESM.

Here are two most common patterns:

1. Expecting extensionless imports to work: e.g. `@aws-sdk/core`

  ```js
  // @aws-sdk/core/dist-es/index.js
  export * from "./submodules/client/index";  // This is expecting to load from @aws-sdk/core/dist-es/submodules/client/index.js
  export * from "./submodules/httpAuthSchemes/index";
  export * from "./submodules/protocols/index";
  ```

  Their package.json looks like this so they are expecting Node.js to always pick up the CJS version:

  ```
   ".": {
      "module": "./dist-es/index.js",
      "node": "./dist-cjs/index.js",
      "import": "./dist-es/index.js",
      "require": "./dist-cjs/index.js",
      "types": "./dist-types/index.d.ts"
    },
  ```
2. Expecting directory imports to work e.g. `@smithy/core`. Their pacakge.json looks similar to 1 with an ESM entry point that looks like this:

  ```js
  // @smithy/core/dist-es/index.js
  export * from "./middleware-http-auth-scheme";  // This is expecting to load from @smithy/core/dist-es/middleware-http-auth-scheme/index.js
  export * from "./middleware-http-signing";
  export * from "./util-identity-and-auth";
  export * from "./getSmithyContext";
  export * from "./normalizeProvider";
  export * from "./protocols/requestBuilder";
  export { createPaginator } from "./pagination/createPaginator";
  ```

I think it might still be worth it to implement `module` as semver-major so that these packages can simply fixup their imports to run bundleless on Node.js - or when they go ESM-only they would have to fix the imports up anyway. But that definitely needs to be semver-major. For this PR I'll just focus on implementing a semver-minor condition with a different name for now.

